{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes\n\n- ID/EX flush now clears only `valid` and the control bundle. Reset still clears the full register, and the payload retains its stall hold behavior. An invalid fetched instruction also captures zero controls.\n- IF masks the fetched instruction only during an imem stall. EX redirect still takes priority for the PC update, and the ID/EX control kill discards the wrong-path instruction.\n- After the narrow bubble change, Gowin timing exposed a regfile-to-dmem path through the multiply result mux. Added a dedicated registered `rs1 + immediate` address for LOAD/STORE and used it for the data-memory address, masks, alignment checks, and load lane selection. This is the one deviation from the proposed file list; it preserves the pipeline and removes the newly exposed arithmetic path from the memory port.\n\nVerification: Verilator lint passed without warnings. The single-channel formal suite passed 53/53 checks on both the narrow bubble change and the final address-path revision. `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and CoreMark. The plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` build defaults to `NRET=2` and fails to compile against this core's declared `nret: 1`; setting `NRET=1` matches the evaluator's configuration.\n\nFinal local FPGA evaluation: median Fmax 57.652 MHz (placements 55.600, 57.652, 57.652 MHz), CoreMark 4,491,485 cycles for 10 iterations, fitness 128.36. The previous accepted divider result in `experiments/log.jsonl` was 48.631 MHz, 4,491,485 cycles, fitness 108.27. No known correctness concerns remain.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
